\description{
\code{distribution_plot()} draws how the states are distributed at each time
point: for every position column it counts the sequences in each state
and stacks the shares (or counts) as areas (default) or bars. It takes the
same inputs as \code{\link[=sequence_plot]{sequence_plot()}} (wide sequences, networks built from
sequences, clusterings, mixture fits and \code{tna} models), draws one panel
per group or cluster, and is the figure \code{sequence_plot(type = "distribution")} draws.

The plot shows the composition of the sample at each time point, not how
individual sequences move: the same bands can come from sequences that
stay in one state or from sequences that change state at every step. Look
at single sequences with \code{\link[=sequence_plot]{sequence_plot()}} and at the transitions with
\code{\link[=build_tna]{build_tna()}}. Missing cells form their own band by default (\code{na}), so
sequences that end early stay visible instead of inflating the other
shares.
